TY  - ART
TI  - Reproducibility spectrum for applied social sciences: conceptual taxonomy and recommended tools
AU  - Rogers, Pablo
AB  - Overview This Zenodo record contains two complementary figures that together provide a comprehensive framework for understanding and implementing reproducibility in Applied Social Sciences research. Adapted from Zaragozi et al. (2020) and aligned with the ARTE (Article Reproducibility Template & Environment) workflow (Rogers et al., 2025; Limongi et al., 2025a, 2025b), these figures support both conceptual understanding and practical implementation of open science practices. Figure 1: Recommended Tools - A practical guide identifying specific technologies commonly used in Applied Social Sciences (e.g., OSF, GitHub, Quarto, Docker). Figure 2: Conceptual Taxonomy - An abstract framework categorizing types of solutions (e.g., "Generalist repositories" instead of "OSF, Zenodo"), enabling discovery of alternatives and adaptation to different contexts. Synergy Between Figures Complementary Functions The two figures work as an integrated pedagogical system: Figure 2 (Taxonomy) answers: "What types of solutions exist for reproducibility?"→ Conceptual understanding, tool-agnostic, future-proof. Figure 1 (Tools) answers: "Which specific tools should I use today?"→ Practical recommendations, ready-to-implement, curated for Applied Social Sciences. Together they provide: Conceptual depth (taxonomy) + Actionable guidance (tools). Flexibility (categories adapt to context) + Specificity (concrete starting points). Longevity (abstract concepts endure) + Immediacy (tools available now). Three-Dimensional Framework Both figures share the same organizational structure: Columns (Components of Reproducibility): Data: Organization, documentation, and sharing of research data. Code: Version control and sharing of analysis scripts. Linked and executable: Integration of data, code, and narrative in computational environments. Rows (Levels of Implementation): Assets: Local tools, technologies, formats, and software. Platforms: Cloud-based services for collaboration, sharing, and archiving. Practices: Methodological approaches for transparency and usability. Color Gradient (Levels of Commitment): 🔴 Red (Wish): Minimal reproducibility - data organization and basic sharing. 🟡 Yellow (Try): Adequate reproducibility - version control + dependency management.→ Recommended zone for most projects 🟢 Green (Commit): Complete reproducibility - computational encapsulation and full automation.→ Aspirational, not mandatory Pedagogical Inversion Unlike the original Zaragozi et al. (2020) spectrum, our adaptation inverts the column order (Data → Code → Linked) to reflect the ARTE learning trajectory: Start with Data (accessible to non-programmers): Organize CSV files, write README, upload to OSF. Progress to Code (introducing programming): Learn Git, version control scripts, share on GitHub. Advance to Linked (computational reproducibility): Integrate with Quarto, manage dependencies, containerize. This progression recognizes that data organization is the entry point for reproducibility in Applied Social Sciences, where many researchers lack programming experience. The Centrality of Practices Why Practices Are Identical in Both Figures The Practices row is the only element that remains unchanged between Figure 1 (Tools) and Figure 2 (Taxonomy). This is intentional and theoretically significant. Key insight: Practices are tool-agnostic and universally applicable. Whether you use GitHub or GitLab (Assets × Code), good version control practices remain the same: ✅ Write descriptive commit messages. ✅ Use branches for experimental work. ✅ Document repository structure in README. ✅ Apply semantic versioning. Whether you publish in OSF or Dataverse (Platforms × Data), good data documentation practices remain the same: ✅ Create data dictionaries. ✅ Include metadata (variable definitions, units, collection methods). ✅ Specify licensing (CC BY, CC0). ✅ Provide usage examples. Practices as "Reproducibility Multiplier" The color gradient reveals a critical hierarchy: Practices × Linked reaches near-maximum green (~95%), while Assets × Linked reaches ~90% and Platforms × Linked reaches ~75%. This demonstrates that methodology is more determinant than technology: ❌ Docker container without Dockerfile or README (Assets without Practices)→ Black box, not reproducible. ✅ Well-documented R scripts with clear Makefile (Practices applied to Code)→ Highly reproducible, even without containers. Implication: A researcher who masters Practices (documentation, automation, licensing) can achieve high reproducibility with modest technology. Conversely, sophisticated technology (Docker, Binder) without practices yields low reproducibility. This challenges the dominant narrative that equates containerization with automatic reproducibility. Our framework shows that literate programming + workflow documentation (Practices × Linked) achieves comparable reproducibility to containers alone (Assets × Linked). Practical Recommendations For Beginners Focus on yellow zone (Adequate reproducibility) Organize data with ARTE. Share data on OSF (Platforms × Data). Learn basic Git (Assets × Code). Write READMEs and data dictionaries (Practices × Data/Code). Don't wait for perfection Sharing organized data is better than sharing nothing. Well-documented scripts without version control are better than undocumented code with Git. For Experienced Researchers Prioritize Practices × Linked Master Quarto for literate programming. Publish your dynamic document on GitHub Pages. Develop automated workflows. Document computational environment (Dockerfile, renv.lock) For Instructors Teach with both figures Start lecture with Figure 2 (conceptual landscape). Assign homework with Figure 1 (concrete tools). Emphasize that Practices row is constant—methodology transcends tools. Technical Specifications Assets Row Differences Dimension Figure 1 (Tools) Figure 2 (Taxonomy) Data Tabular data, Public APIs, Text/qualitative data, Relational databases Data formats, Data sources, Qualitative data, Databases Code R, Python, Git, RStudio, Jupyter, JASP, jamovi, Zotero Statistical interfaces, Programming languages, Development environment, Version control Linked Quarto, RMarkdown, Environment packages, Docker, Podman Dynamic documents, Dependency managers, Containerization technologies Interpretation: Figure 1 names specific tools; Figure 2 categorizes tool types. Platforms Row Differences Dimension Figure 1 (Tools) Figure 2 (Taxonomy) Data OSF, Zenodo, Dataverse, ICPSR Institutional repositories, Generalist repositories, Disciplinary repositories Code GitHub, GitLab, OSF, Zenodo Integrated platforms, Collaborative versioning, Permanent archiving Linked GitHub Pages, Quarto Pub, Binder, Posit Cloud, Google Colab Static hosting, Online notebooks, Executable repositories Interpretation: Figure 1 recommends specific platforms; Figure 2 groups platforms by function and scope. Practices Row (Identical in Both) Dimension Content (Same in Both Figures) Data Metadata, Data documentation, Licensing Code Code documentation, Version practices, Licensing Linked Literate programming, Workflow automation, Process modeling Interpretation: Best practices are universal, independent of tool choice. Use Cases Research Project planning: Select appropriate tools/practices for project requirements. Collaboration: Establish shared workflows with co-authors. Manuscript preparation: Document computational methods for transparency. Education Course design: Structure syllabi around the three-column progression. Workshops: Quick-reference guides for tool selection. Thesis advising: Set reproducibility expectations for graduate students. Individual Learning Self-assessment: Identify current position on spectrum. Skill development: Plan progressive learning path. Tool exploration: Discover alternatives within each category. AI Use Statement The figures were entirely conceived and created by the author. Claude 4.5 Sonnet (Anthropic, December 4-5, 2025) was used as an assistive tool to draft and refine the descriptive documentation, under the author's full supervision and curation. The AI supported text structuring, elaboration of theoretical justifications, and synthesis of explanatory content. Following AI use, the author critically reviewed, substantially edited, and validated all content. The author assumes full responsibility for the intellectual contributions, accuracy, and integrity of this publication. All conceptual and creative decisions were made by the author; the AI served exclusively as a writing assistant. This statement follows guidelines by Sampaio et al. (2024) for ethical AI use in research.
